Feature: dalogin connection pool implementation analysis (Agroal vs HikariCP)

Contract IDs
- None (analysis and decision feature; no runtime contract change)

Overview
- Evaluate whether `dalogin-quarkus` should keep the Quarkus-native Agroal pool or adopt HikariCP.
- Produce a recorded, evidence-backed decision rather than an implementation.
- Establish the pool-tuning baseline that the other three split features assume.

Split context
- This is part 4 of the `dalogin-datasource-pooling` split recorded in
  `speckit.system.refactoring-mentions-roadmap.plan`.
- Analysis-only. It does not block parts 1-3, and parts 1-3 do not depend on its outcome
  beyond confirming that configuration keys stay valid.

Role collaboration
- `@backend-dev`, `@performance-engineer`, `@testing-engineer`

Baseline (as-found)
- `dalogin-quarkus/pom.xml` declares `quarkus-jdbc-mysql`, which supplies Agroal as the managed pool.
- No HikariCP dependency is present in the module today.
- Current configuration: `min-size=5`, `max-size=20`, `acquisition-timeout` commented out.
- The module also uses `quarkus-opentelemetry` and `quarkus-micrometer-registry-prometheus`,
  so pool observability is already wired through the Quarkus stack.
- Historical `c3p0` knowledge exists in `.ai/skills/hibernate-session-c3p0-debug/`, which is
  `mbooks`/Hibernate-oriented and not a dalogin pooling decision.

Key files
- `dalogin-quarkus/pom.xml`
- `dalogin-quarkus/src/main/resources/application.properties`
- `k8infra/quarkus-backend.yaml`
- `.ai/memory/k8s.yaml`

Analysis requirements
- `AR-001`: Document how Agroal integrates with Quarkus (configuration, health checks, metrics,
  dev services, and Narayana JTA transaction management).
- `AR-002`: Document what adopting HikariCP in Quarkus would require, including whether a supported
  extension exists or a manual `DataSource` producer would be needed.
- `AR-003`: Identify Quarkus features that would be lost or hand-rolled under a manual HikariCP producer.
- `AR-004`: Identify any concrete, observed problem that HikariCP would solve for dalogin.
- `AR-005`: Compare native-image and build-time implications for both options.
- `AR-006`: Compare metrics exposure through the existing Micrometer/Prometheus pipeline.
- `AR-007`: Capture a pool-behavior baseline under the existing JMeter load plan.
- `AR-008`: Recommend concrete `min-size`, `max-size`, and `acquisition-timeout` values with rationale.
- `AR-009`: Produce an explicit keep-or-migrate recommendation with supporting evidence.

Decision criteria
- Whether a real, measured pooling deficiency exists in dalogin.
- Integration cost with Quarkus transaction management and health/metrics.
- Operational consistency with `mbook-quarkus` and `mbooks-quarkus`.
- Maintenance burden of a hand-managed pool versus a framework-managed one.

Expected default position
- Agroal is the Quarkus-native, transaction-integrated pool and is already active.
- The analysis should therefore treat "keep Agroal and tune it" as the default outcome, and require
  positive evidence before recommending HikariCP.

Out of scope
- Implementing a pool migration.
- Changing datasource credentials or externalizing secrets.
- Tuning MySQL server-side connection limits.
- `mbooks-quarkus` Hibernate session/pool behavior.

Acceptance criteria
- `AC-001`: A written comparison covering `AR-001` through `AR-006` exists.
- `AC-002`: A measured pool baseline under load exists.
- `AC-003`: Recommended pool settings are stated with rationale.
- `AC-004`: A clear keep-or-migrate decision is recorded.
- `AC-005`: If the decision is to keep Agroal, the HikariCP option is documented as evaluated and
  declined so it is not re-litigated informally.
